  "live_records": {
    "q-65233": {
      "dom_id": "step-q65233",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "$(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we expand the parentheses and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1) & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & =-(1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the identity $1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ to rewrite the expression on the right-hand side. \n\n$$\n-(1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )=-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)=-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
