      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "$\\operatorname{sec}⁡x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{cos}^{3}⁡x)=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{cos}^{3}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot {cos}^{3}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the identity $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ to further simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot {cos}^{3}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot {cos}^{3}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}-\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the identity ${sin}^{2}⁡x=1-{cos}^{2}⁡x$ to rewrite the expression on the right-hand side.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{cos}^{3}⁡x)={sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
