    "q-65291": {
      "dom_id": "step-q65291",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Simplify the expression $\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the numerator using $1-{cos}^{2}⁡x={sin}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}=\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the numerator as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1} & =\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}}{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & =-(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=-(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
